  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nAn upper shortwave trough is currently sliding across the Central \nPlains into the Middle Mississippi Valley. At the surface, expansive \nhigh pressure sits over the Great Lakes and extends into the \nNorthern Plains. As we move into tonight, drizzle is possible to \ndevelop mainly in northern to central KS where weak CAA is expected \nto be. Chances are low (around 15%) with these chances continuing \ninto Tuesday morning. For Tuesday, cloud cover is expected to \ndissipate into the afternoon with high temperatures near normal in \nthe mid to upper 70s across the area. Mostly dry conditions are \nexpected for Tuesday into Wednesday as the surface high pressure \nexpands into the Central Plains. There is another slight chance for \nrain mainly in north central KS on Wednesday morning, though most of \nthe area will remain dry. Highs for Wednesday afternoon are forecast \naround 80 across our area.  \n\nAs we move into late Wednesday night, an upper shortwave trough is \nprogged to move into the Northern/Central Plains. This will increase \nrain chances over western Kansas Thursday morning with activity \nanticipated to move east into our area during the day Thursday as \nsoutherly flow advects better moisture into Kansas. Showers and weak \nthunderstorms are forecast with this activity as most guidance keeps \ninstability values under 500 J/kg with weak wind shear. Periodic \nrain chances are possible from Thursday night through Saturday as \nadditional shortwaves eject out from the Rockies and slide across \nthe region. Warmer temperatures are progged to return this weekend \nas above normal 850 mb temperatures are expected. High temperatures \nare forecast to be in the mid to upper 80s for Saturday and\nSunday.",
